    {
      "correction_id": 82,
      "source": "SafeLearning/primer-basics.html",
      "before_sha256": "eda0c7f3df932ab0449f6d501bb1260350ba3086229249d68b884e1767048357",
      "after_sha256": "f9b6d6adb95fb3c261768d3dc581046f7bc13e5d8c8223fd52e8ae219c68a300",
      "replacements": [
        {
          "before": "Errors $e_k\\to0$ converge",
          "after": "Nonnegative errors $e_k\\to0$ converge",
          "actual_current_occurrences": 1
        },
        {
          "before": "typically $e_k\\le C/k^p$;",
          "after": "for example $e_k=C/k^p$ with $C,p\\gt0$ and $k\\ge1$ (a polynomial upper bound alone does not rule out geometric convergence);",
          "actual_current_occurrences": 1
        },
        {
          "before": "if $e_{k+1}\\le Ce_k^2$ (Newton's method near a solution).",
          "after": "if $e_{k+1}\\le Ce_k^2$ with $C\\gt0$ (scalar Newton's method near a regular root of a $C^2$ function, from a sufficiently close initial point).",
          "actual_current_occurrences": 1
        },
        {
          "before": "Reaching $e_k\\le\\varepsilon$ takes $k\\ge\\ln(e_0/\\varepsilon)/\\ln(1/q)$ iterations at a linear rate, growing like $\\ln(1/\\varepsilon)$, but $k\\ge1/\\varepsilon^2$ for $1/\\sqrt k$. For $\\varepsilon=10^{-6}$, $e_0=1$: $20$ iterations at $q=0.5$ against $10^{12}$. On a logarithmic plot a linear rate is a straight line, a sublinear one flattens (see the explorer).",
          "after": "For $e_0,\\varepsilon\\gt0$, the geometric envelope $q^ke_0$ is at most $\\varepsilon$ iff $k\\ge\\ln(e_0/\\varepsilon)/\\ln(1/q)$; this cutoff suffices for any errors $e_k\\le q^ke_0$, and grows like $\\ln(1/\\varepsilon)$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ with fixed $q\\in(0,1)$ and $e_0$. For the exact errors $e_k=1/\\sqrt k$, $k\\ge1$, reaching the tolerance is equivalent to $k\\ge1/\\varepsilon^2$. At $\\varepsilon=10^{-6}$ and $e_0=1$, the two exact envelopes first reach the tolerance at $20$ iterations for $q=0.5$ and at $10^{12}$, respectively; faster errors can reach it earlier. On a logarithmic plot exact positive geometric errors form a straight line, while the logarithmic slope of a positive reciprocal-power curve tends to zero (see the explorer).",
          "actual_current_occurrences": 1
        }
      ],
      "corrected_at_utc": "2026-10-12T23:33:18.575868+00:00",
